\subsection{Observation 2: behavioral facts recur but are not identified by the candidate}
\label{sec:obs-identity}
Measuring $J$ requires a behavioral execution for every emitted candidate whose label is not yet known. Version comparisons repeat many of these executions. SEW embeds a keyed style watermark into Python sources~\cite{sew2026}, and we evaluate four versions of it on the same tasks and keys. On sanitized MBPP, the stock and S4 versions together produce 2,562 emitted rows. Forty of them are byte-identical to their qualified references and need no execution; the remaining 2,522 rows contain only 1,138 distinct obligations, because identical candidates recur across keys and versions. The duplication is structural: a version that changes a rare transformation leaves most outputs byte-identical.

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
Watermarking is one of the main proposals for tracing code produced or distributed by language models. A code watermark rewrites a program, or biases its generation, so that a detector can later recognize the program as marked~\cite{lee2024,yang2023,acw2026}. The rewrite is acceptable only if the marked program still does what the original was supposed to do. A watermark that is reliably detected in broken programs is of little use to the developer who receives them, and a watermark that preserves behavior but is rarely detected cannot support provenance claims. The quantity that matters for a deployment is therefore paired: the share of scheduled attempts that emit a program that is both behaviorally acceptable and detected.

